00:02:48.140 --> 00:02:50.800
 Okay, but now we're going to do this in Python.

00:02:50.800 --> 00:02:52.940
 And so here's the code we're going to write.

00:02:52.940 --> 00:02:55.100
 So we're going to import the socket library

00:02:55.100 --> 00:02:56.520
 and we're going to make a socket.

00:02:56.520 --> 00:02:58.240
 Now this doesn't actually make a connection.

00:02:58.240 --> 00:03:00.380
 Think of a socket as a file handle

00:03:00.380 --> 00:03:03.200
 that doesn't have any data associated with it yet.

00:03:03.200 --> 00:03:05.380
 And then what we're going to do is we're going to reach out

00:03:05.380 --> 00:03:09.540
 and connect that socket to a destination across the internet

00:03:09.540 --> 00:03:13.580
 with the domain name of data.pr4e.org.

00:03:13.580 --> 00:03:15.360
 And the second parameter in this tuple,

00:03:15.360 --> 00:03:19.580
 this is a function call with a single tuple as a parameter.

00:03:19.580 --> 00:03:22.180
 And so tuple sub zero is data.pr4e.org

00:03:22.180 --> 00:03:23.600
 and tuple sub one is the 80,

00:03:23.600 --> 00:03:26.480
 which says I want to talk to port 80.

00:03:26.480 --> 00:03:28.620
 That could fail.

00:03:28.620 --> 00:03:32.780
 It will make the connection and if the port 80 is there,

00:03:32.780 --> 00:03:34.640
 away it goes.

00:03:34.640 --> 00:03:37.160
 And then we're going to actually send the HTTP command.

00:03:37.160 --> 00:03:39.580
 So get, this is the HTTP rules,

00:03:39.580 --> 00:03:43.040
 followed by an end of line, followed by a blank line.

00:03:43.040 --> 00:03:44.400
 So you saw me do this there.

00:03:44.400 --> 00:03:45.960
 This was what I typed here.

00:03:45.960 --> 00:03:47.280
 And then I had to type a blank line.

00:03:47.280 --> 00:03:50.320
 Now if you want to go read the RFCs for how to do this,

00:03:50.320 --> 00:03:51.800
 you can figure this out.

00:03:51.800 --> 00:03:53.940
 So the only other thing that's kind of weird here

00:03:53.940 --> 00:03:56.940
 is we have to add this dot in code.

00:03:56.940 --> 00:04:01.680
 And that's because there are strings inside of Python

00:04:01.680 --> 00:04:04.500
 that are in Unicode and we have to send them out

00:04:04.500 --> 00:04:06.520
 as what's called UTF-8.

00:04:06.520 --> 00:04:10.260
 And in code converts from Unicode internally to UTF-8.

00:04:10.260 --> 00:04:14.260
 So this command is a set of UTF-8 bytes

00:04:14.260 --> 00:04:15.600
 that we're then going to send.

00:04:15.600 --> 00:04:18.820
 It still has that same set of characters in it

00:04:18.820 --> 00:04:19.820
 and now we're going to send it.

00:04:19.820 --> 00:04:23.220
 And that's, after we've made the connection,

00:04:23.220 --> 00:04:24.680
 we're going to send these two things

00:04:24.680 --> 00:04:26.780
 and then we're going to wait.

00:04:26.780 --> 00:04:29.700
 And my SOC is like a file handle at that point

00:04:29.700 --> 00:04:31.980
 because it's been opened and we've sent data.

00:04:31.980 --> 00:04:34.940
 The HTTP protocol told us what we had to send

00:04:34.940 --> 00:04:36.700
 and the fact that we did have to send it.

00:04:36.700 --> 00:04:39.000
 So now I have just a simple while loop

00:04:39.000 --> 00:04:42.880
 and I'm going to ask up to 512 characters

00:04:42.880 --> 00:04:47.040
 and receive up to 512 characters and get that back.

00:04:47.040 --> 00:04:49.980
 If I will know that this is the end of file

00:04:49.980 --> 00:04:52.900
 if I got no data back, so if the length of the data,

00:04:52.900 --> 00:04:55.660
 the byte array that I got back is less than one,

00:04:55.660 --> 00:04:56.860
 then I'm going to quit.

00:04:56.860 --> 00:04:58.220
 Otherwise, I'm going to print the data

00:04:58.220 --> 00:04:59.420
 and I'm going to use this decode,

00:04:59.420 --> 00:05:01.660
 which is kind of the opposite of this encode.

00:05:01.660 --> 00:05:06.100
 What I'm getting is UTF-8 encoded data, most likely,

00:05:06.100 --> 00:05:09.220
 and decode basically converts it to the internal format

00:05:09.220 --> 00:05:11.820
 called Unicode that runs inside.

00:05:11.820 --> 00:05:13.300
 So this is going to run a bunch of times,

00:05:13.300 --> 00:05:15.860
 pulling in the blocks, basically 512,

00:05:15.860 --> 00:05:18.820
 up to 512 characters at a time, printing it out,

00:05:18.820 --> 00:05:20.080
 and then when it's all said and done,

00:05:20.080 --> 00:05:21.900
 we will close that connection.

00:05:21.900 --> 00:05:24.640
 And so it's not too exciting.

